## ABSTRACT
Metal-organicframeworks(MOFs)withHKUST-ike tbo structures havebeen paid specificattentionfor gassortion and separation because of their specic pore features.According to the geometric similarity of spirobifluorene and $[\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{O}_{2}\mathsf{C R})_{4}]$ paddlewheelsecondarybuilding units (Cu2SBUs)inHKUST-1,we attempted torationallconstructaHKUST-like MOFbya substitutionstrategy.Usinga judiciouslydesignedoctatopiccarboxylateligandacopper-organicframework,JUC-20was synthesized.Thecrystalsof JUC-220exhibitedcharacteristic features incubicwithdisorderposiblyduetothedisorder substitution and highsymmetry of tbotopology.TworelatedHKUST-ike structuremodels were considered.Thanks to the suitable pore size and specific pore shapes, the adsorption selectivities of JUC-220 for $C_{3}H_{8}/C H_{4}$ $(5/85)$ and $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ (10/85) gas mixtures were as high as 736 and 46 respectively at $298~\mathsf{K}$ and 1 bar. Specially, JUC-220 exhibited excellent trace adsorption performance of $C_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{6}$ as well as reverse adsorption behavior of $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ . Thus, JUC-220 serves as an example ofHKUST-likeMOFwithdisorderforlighthydrocarbons separationandtheimplementationofsubstitutionwhichcanbe used to explore more porous MOFs.
## KEYWORDS
HKUST-like metal-organicframeworks (MOFs),reticularchemistry,disorder,substitution,trace adsorption, light hydrocarbons separation
## Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline microporous materials self-assembled from metal ions or clusters and organic ligands with various topological nets [1]. Owing to their high surface areas and crystallinity, as well as their tunable pore features (size, shape, and inner surface with adsorption sites), MOFs have been intensively explored for gas storage and separations [2,3]. Meanwhile, reticular chemistry is an attractive and effective tool to synthesize MOFs with desired structures or properties [4,5]. However, the rational construction of targeted MOFs remains a substantial challenge, especially by highly connected organic linkers (e.g., with 6-8 connectors) [6]. Firstly, the highly connected organic linkers are usually difficult to synthesize because they have larger molecular volume and weight with more branches and coordinating groups. Secondly, the large molecular volume and weight of highly connected organic ligands usually brings a dissolution problem when using them to assemble MOFs under solvothermal method. Lastly, the biggest challenge is that the structure of highly connect organic ligands in solution is more flexible and harder to control during MOF formation because of their rotatable multi-branches. All these drawbacks could lead to the difficulty of crystallization of regular MOFs and prevent us from the structural solution of MOFs by single-crystal X-ray diffraction (XRD) [7,8]. On the other hand, MOFs exhibiting interesting types of structural disorder, including topological disorder, static disorder, dynamic disorder, and low-dimensional disorder, are attracting increasing attention owing that they offer a platform to understand the formation of these complex disorder structures and also whether it might be implicated in useful functionalities [9, 10].
The separation of light hydrocarbons, especially Cl-3 hydrocarbons, is very challenging and relies almost exclusively on high-pressure cryogenic distillation because of their low boiling points and small molecular sizes [3]. Adsorption-based methods based on selective adsorption by the adsorbent, usually porous solids, could replace the thermally driven separation techniques, and significantly reduce the energy consumption as well as the cost of manufacturing these common important chemicals. The properties of solid adsorbents (composition, surface polarity, pore structure, etc.) manifestly play a key role in this developing technology and largely determine the final separation eficiency. Among a large amount of research on MOFs focusing on the storage and separation of light hydrocarbons, MOFs with tbo topology (the canonical HKUST-1 firstly [11]） constructed with $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(O_{2}C R)_{4}]}$ paddlewheel secondary building units (Cu2 SBUs) and multicarboxylate ligands have been paid specific attention mainly because of their specific porous frameworks with two kinds of cages and open metal sites. HKUST-1 was reported as a good benchmarking compound for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ sorption at ambient conditions [l2],and firstly examined for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation [13]. One of HKUST-like tbo-MOFs, Cu-tbo-MOF-5 was reported in 20l6 to behave the best performing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage among all the MOFs at that time [14]. In contrast to other MOFs with exposed metal sites for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption, HKUST-l and HKUST-like MOFs have additional strong adsorption sites located in the windows of each octahedral cage owing to several close interactions to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ matched with suitable distances and symmetries [14-16].
Herein, we designed and synthesized a new octatopic carboxylic ligand with central spirobifluorene (SBF） core and four isophthalate branches, namely $5{,}5{,}5{^{\circ}}{,}5{^{\circ}}{^{\prime}}{)}$ -(9,9'-spirobi[fluorene]- $^{2,2^{\circ},7,7^{\circ}}$ -tetrayl)tetraisophthalic acid (HgSFTTP). The synthetic procedures of $\mathrm{H_{8}S F T T P}$ were shown (Section S3.l in the ESM). The SBF-core is with very specific rigid geometry, constituted of two orthogonal fluorene units connected through a shared spiro carbon. Therefore, it could be designed as 4-connected (4-c) square node with phenyl rings perpendicular to the square [21], which is different from other 4-c square ligands [22,23]. Furthermore,we notice that the geometry and size of SBF are highly similar to the classic $\mathrm{Cu}2$ SBUs in HKUST-1(Fig. S2 in the ESM). We conceived that this octatopic ligand could form a framework with a portion of inorganic $\mathrm{Cu}2$ SBUs in HKUST-1 replaced by the organic segment SBFs, since we had done a similar replacement in the case of JUC-l0o based on the classic MOF-5 [24]. Using the ligand $\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{SFTTP}$ we synthesized a copper-organic framework (named JUC-220, JUC $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ Jilin University, China) (Scheme 1). The crystals of JUC-220 were revealed to be twins or/and polymorphs in cubic, and two possible HKUST-like phases in JUC-220 were considered. The potential use of JUC-220 for gas separation was investigated, which demonstrated that JUC-220 exhibited highly  selective gas  adsorption performance of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ as well as trace adsorption performance of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$
## 2 Results and discussion
SBF and its derivatives have been reported as chiral ligands and electropolymerizable building blocks, and they play a key role in the development of biomedicine, organic electronics, and solidstate laser [25]. However, the use of SBF-core ligands for the synthesis of crystalline porous MOFs attracted more attention only in recent few years [26-32]. And there are only no more than fifty coordination polymers reported based on SBF-core ligands, although few them showed permanent porosity and the number of structures in the MOF subset of the Cambridge Structural Database (CSD) is over ll0,000 [33], probably because of the low crystallization ability of SBF-based compounds. On the other side, SBF-based monomers have been reported for the synthesis of porous organic polymers for optoelectronic applications and gas separation [34-37]. In this work, the key motivation for design of the dendritic ligand $\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{SFTTP}$ is the geometric similarity of SBF and the Cu2 SBUs in HKUST-l,thus to obtain a HKUST-like framework by substitution and modified pore environment for gas separation.
To implement our design strategy, solvothermal reaction between $\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{SFTTP}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in a mixed solvent of $\mathrm{N,N^{\prime}}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF） and ammonia solution was performed. The reaction yielded a homogeneous crystalline material JUC-220. Optical microscopy and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) demonstrate that JUC-220 exhibits uniform rhombic dodecahedral morphology which is for typical cubic crystals expressing $\{110\}$ crystal facets (Figs. 1(a) and 1(b)) [38]. The crystals were green and with size from several micrometers to around 100 micrometers. Sequentially, we attempted to solve the structure by single crystal X-ray diffraction. The diffraction patterns provided strong and periodic spots indicating the high crystallinity of JUC-220 (Fig.1(c) and Fig.S6 in the ESM). The spots are clear and appear to be without any elongation, indicating the crystal is not badly splitting. After three-dimensional (3D) reciprocal lattice was constructed, the indexing of observed reflections identified a body-centered cubic Bravais lattice with two dominant domains and gave a unit cell with $a=b=c=$ $35.87\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Fig.1(d) and Figs. S7 and S8 in the ESM). But next, we had a serious problem when reducing and integrating the diffraction data, since the average correlation coeficient values of spot shape were very low which means that most of the spots were not perfect sphere and many reflections observed were overlapped, suggesting the intrinsic property of twinning in JUC220 (Fig.S9 in the ESM). Moreover, the complexity of twinning on cubic crystals in crystallography prevented us from a successful structure solution [8,39]. The polymorphism of JUC-220 might arise from the quasi-planar SBF core which is with pseudo high symmetry as the Cu2 SBUs or/and the similar but different geometrical size of SBF core to ${\mathrm{Cu}}2\ {\mathrm{SBUs}},$ thus leading to some degree of disorder in the substitution of organic segments for inorganic SBUs.
To elucidate the structure, we noticed that the unit cell parameters of JUC-220 correlate with that of some tbo-MOFs, such as MFM-180 $(a=b=18.69\mathrm{~\AA}.$ $c=35.9\mathrm{~\AA~}$ : space group: $I\bar{4}2m\dot{}$ ）[18]. What's more, the unit cell length of $35.87\mathrm{\AA}$ for JUC220 approximates to the [ll0] period of the cubic HKUST-1 structure. Considering the reason for the design of $\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{SFTTP}$ mentioned above, we constructed two structural models based on the structure of HKUST-1. It is worth mentioning that all the reported HKUST-like MOFs based on Cu2 SBUs and tetraisophthalate linkers are with the same topology to tbo-MOFs by Eddaoudi et al. so far. This kind of binodal tbo-derived net can transform to scu topology if the linkers are regarded as 8-c nodes (Figs. 2(a) and $2(\mathrm{c})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ . During our quest, we recognized that the replacement of Cu2 SBUs in HKUST-1 by organic segments in our strategy could also lead to another binodal tbo-derived net, which could transform to another (4,8)-c net urj topology when the linkers act as 8-c nodes (Figs. 2(b) and 2(d)). Based on these two binodal tbo-derived nets and with the help of unit cell parameters obtained from single crystal X-ray diffraction, we generated the structural models, JUC-220-scu and JUC-220-urj (Figs.2(e) and 2(f) respectively). These two isomeric structures are both in tetragonal crystal system, but different space groups ${\bar{I}}{\bar{4}}2m$ for JUC-220-scu and $P\bar{4}n2$ for JUC-220-urj respectively). These structure models were geometrically optimized and both of them exhibited reasonable assembly of Cu2 SBUs and SFTTP linkers. It is supposed that the crystals of JUC-220 were twins or/and polymorphs of several domain states including JUC-220-scu, JUC220-urj or/and other unexpected ones.
The phase of JUC-220 was characterized by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). As shown in Fig.3(a), the experimental PXRD pattern of as-synthesized JUC-220 sample shows characteristic peaks. And then,we tried to index the peaks based on the lattice information obtained from single-crystal diffraction. It is found that the space group Ia3d (no. 230) is mostly in accordance with the existing peaks in the PXRD pattern (Fig. S13 in the ESM). The signals at $6.92^{\circ}$ $9.18^{\circ}$ 。 $9.8^{\circ}$ 。 $12.02^{\circ}$ $15.9^{\circ}$ 。 $17.8^{\circ}{\mathrm{:}}$ and $19.38^{\circ}$ correspond to the (220),(321),(400), (422),(541), (640),and (732) crystal planes. However, there are still few peaks which don't agree with the lattice, including two small peaks at ${6.4^{\circ}}$ and $6.56^{\circ}$ as well as the strong peak at $11.8^{\circ}$ which is very close to the peak at $12.02^{\circ}:$ demonstrating the disorder in the structure of JUC-220. Subsequently, we compared the PXRD pattern of JUC-220 with the simulated patterns of HKUST-1, JUC-220-scu, and JUC-220-urj. As shown in Fig. 3(a), the PXRD pattern of JUC220 is close to these patterns, especially that of HKUST-1, although their unit cell parameters are not the same. In detail, the diffraction pattern of JUC-220 behaves more peaks than that of HKUST-1 in cubic and fewer peaks than those of JUC-220-scu and JUC-220-urj in tetragonal crystal system, because the number of reflections is prone to decrease with higher symmetry theoretically. The disappearance of some peaks from patterns of JUC-220-scu and JUC-220-urj including the strong peak at $\sim5^{\circ}$ in JUC-220 pattern is probably due to the disorder in the framework, diffraction overlap or/and different systematic absences of reflections because of higher pseudo-symmetry in JUC-220. To reveal more structural information, Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) measurements were performed (Fig.S14 in the ESM). Compared with FT-IR pattern of $\mathrm{H}_{8}{\mathrm{SFTTP}}$ the pattern of JUC-220 shows that the peaks at ca. l240 and ca. $3000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which could be contributed to isolated hydroxyls almost diminished, indicating the deprotonation of the ligands in JUC220 was accomplished [40].
To access the guest solvents of JUC-220, we implemented thermogravimetricanalysis (TGA） for JUC-220. JUC-220 experienced three different stages of weight loss when the temperature gradually increased (Fig.Sl5 in the ESM). About 25.8 wt.% loss before $340~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ could be contributed to the department of guest DMF and water molecules. And then, the skeleton of JUC-220 began to sharply colapse. The decomposition finished at ca. $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm at $77\mathrm{K}$ for JUC-220 was measured to examine its permanent porosity. Before the measurement, the sample was solvent-exchanged with methanol and dichloromethane in sequence, and then evacuated at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ As shown in Fig. 3(b), JUC-220 behaves a typical type-I $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm as expected for microporous material. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and pore volume were calculated to be $828\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.375\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ ，Pore size distribution (PSD) was also calculated using non-local density functional theory (NLDFT). The corresponding curve shows a high and narrow peak at $5.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (insert in Fig. 3(b)), which agrees with the window size of simulated structural models. There are also some small and broad peaks on the PSD curve, of which the highest centered at $11.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ could correspond to the size of cage in the simulated models and others might origin from the disorder of JUC-220. It should be noted that the surface area of JUC-220 is lower than HKUST-1 and other HKUST-like MOFs. And the PSD of JUC-220 is dominated by the window size while HKUST1 and other HKUST-like MOFs are mostly dominated by their cage size, which might be caused by its highly twinning structure and lead to the decrease of surface area of JUC-220. In order to explore the stability of JUC-220,we measured the PXRD patterns of JUC-220 after activation and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption measurement and the second cycle of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption at 77 K (Figs. Sl6 and S17 in the ESM). It can be seen that after activation and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption measurements, the peaks of PXRD patterns at $6.4^{\circ}{\mathrm{:}}$ $6.56^{\circ}$ ，and $11.8^{\circ}$ which do not agree to the crystal lattice were diminished, while the peak at $11.06^{\circ}$ corresponding to the (420) crystal plane appeared. This phenomenon might indicate that the department of solvents in as-synthesized JUC-220 reduced the disorder in JUC220 crystals. Almost no change was observed in the comparison of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms, indicating that JUC-220 retained its good crystallinity and porosity. We also checked the PXRD pattern of JUC-220 treated in water for 3 weeks (Fig.Sl8 in the ESM). The maintenance of key peaks indicated its stability in water.
As the kinetic diameters of Cl-3 hydrocarbons and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ which commonly presents in the production and utilization of light hydrocarbons are in the $3{-}5\mathrm{\AA}$ range,the desirable pore size and intrinsic permanent porosity of JUC-220 promoted us to further investigate its potential application for light hydrocarbons separation. Single-component $\mathrm{CH}_{4},\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6},\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8},\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption isotherms for JUC-220 at 298 and 273 K up to 1.0 bar were measured. All the gas adsorptions showed reversible type I isotherms. As shown in Fig.4(a) and Fig.S19 in the ESM, the adsorption of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H}_{6}$ increases sharply in low pressure region ( $(<0.05$ bar) for both isotherms at 298 and $273\mathrm{K},$ while the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption is low and shows almost linear isotherm. These results indicate that there exists higher adsorption affinity of the JUC-220 skeleton towards $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ as well as ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ respectively compared to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ The $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}},$ $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption also showed concave isotherms and higher uptakes compared to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (Fig.4(b) and Fig.S2O in the ESM). The $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption demonstrated close capacity in contrast to $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6},$ and both of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ were higher than that of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ It should be noted that the order of gases that MOF materials prefer to adsorb is typically $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}>\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}>\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ owing to their decreasing polarity [4l]. The $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ -selective adsorption behavior of JUC-220 for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation benefits to avoid multi-cycles of adsorption and desorption when producing high purity $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ which is  an important issue in industry.
To quantitatively evaluate the binding strength of examined gases in JUC-220, their isosteric heats of adsorption $(Q_{s t})$ on JUC220 under various loadings were estimated using  the Clausius-Clapeyron equation following fitting of the adsorption isotherm data at 273 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ using a virial equation [42]. The $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ lie in the range $21.5{-}23.0\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ while for $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{n}$ $(n\ =\ 2,\ 4,\ 6)$ ，and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ these values are in the ranges 28.9-30.2, 33.9-39.0,and $41.6{-}50.1~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ respectively (Figs. S21-S32 in the ESM). The sequence of the ensuing enthalpies is $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}>\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\approx\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\approx\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}>\mathrm{CO_{2}}>\mathrm{CH_{4}},$ which is consistent with a supposable consequence based on the isotherms. Compared to other MOFs, the $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ on JUC-220 is moderate [43],whereas those for alkanes are higher than many reported MOFs such as MFM-202a [44], $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ [45],and UTSA-35a[46],compared to BSF-2 [47],SBMOF-2[48]，and ZUL-C1 [49], less than ZUL-C2 [49]. The high binding strength of JUC-220 to alkanes was plausibly owing to the pore features of HKUST-like MOFs offering close and strong interactions to hydrocarbons, and especially their additional triangle windows of octahedral cages could provide special interaction to methyl groups with 3-axis symmetry [l6]. It should be noted that the similarity of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}},$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H}_{6}$ enthalpies was possibly due to the trade-of between the effects of suitable pore features of JUC220 for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption [50] and higher polarity of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ than $\mathrm{C_{2}H}_{6}$ [51].
The efficient separations of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ are prerequisites for fully utilizing the resources and updating the quality of natural gas [4l]. Pipeline natural gas is a hydrocarbons gas mixture, consisting of 70 wt.%-96 wt.% $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ 0 wt.%-20 wt.% ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6},$ 0.01 $\mathrm{wt.\%-5\wt.\%}$ $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8},}$ trace amounts of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{i}}$ etc.[52].The achievement of ultrapure $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is highly necessary for the reduction of low emission of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ cost-efficient delivery of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in pipeline, and efficient conversion of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ into value-added product [41]. Meanwhile, the separated individual ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ gases are widely needed in their cracking processes to generate alkene respectively [4l]. The strong adsorption affinities of the pores in JUC-220 towards $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ based on the above analysis indicate that it could adsorb trace amounts of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ from the gas mixture of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ .At $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.01,0.03, and 0.05 bar, the $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ adsorption capacities for JUC-220 are 1.93, 2.37, and $2.55\mathrm{mmol/g}$ respectively (Fig. $4(\mathrm{c})\rangle$ ,while they are 2.58, 2.86, and $2.99\mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ respectively, indicating the high adsorption abilities of JUC-220 for $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ at low pressure. Compared to $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ adsorption, the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption for JUC-220 is with relatively weak adsorption affinity, and the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption capacity for JUC-220 is $2.06\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at 0.1 bar and $298\ \mathrm{K},$ while it is $2.81\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $273{\mathrm{~K}}.$ In harsh contrast, the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorptions for JUC-220 at 0.1 bar are only 0.20 and $0.42\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}$ at 298 and $273\mathrm{K}$ respectively. These results may indicate the highly selective $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ or $\mathrm{C_{2}H}_{6}$ adsorptions from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$
The separation performance of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8},}$ ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6},$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ by JUC220 was investigated by the calculation of adsorption selectivities using ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST). The adsorption data of these gases were fitted with the dual-Langmuir-Freundlich isotherm model, while $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption data were fitted with a single-site Langmuir model [45]. As shown in Fig.4(d), the adsorption selectivities of JUC-220 for $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C H_{4}}$ (5/85）and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ (10/85) gas mixtures were as high as 736 and 46 respectively at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar. It is worth noting that these values are both higher than all existing MOFs under the same condition except the polycycloalkane-carboxylate MOF, ZUL-C2 [53],which was reported very recently (Fig.4(e) and Table S7 in the ESM). We also calculated the adsorption selectivities of equimolar $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ for JUC-220 which are 873 and 39 respectively and compared to more reported MOFs (Table S8 in the ESM). It was found that the $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity is higher than most reported MOFs under the same condition, such as JUC100 to -103 [54],Mg-MOF-74 [15], UiO-67 [55],FIR-C1 [56], and JLU-MOF51 [57] with large pores, UTSA-35a [46], MOF-801 [58],FJI-C4 [59],SNNU-Bai68[52],and JLU-Liu21 [60] with small pores, and even BSF-1 [61] with free anions, compared to JLU-Liu40 [62],only lower than BSF-2 [47],MIL-142A [63],and Ni(HBTC)(bipy) [64]. Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity of JUC-220 is also higher than most reported MOFs, only lower than BSF-2 [47] and ZUL-C2 [53], and compared to FJI-C4 [59] (Table S8 in the ESM). The $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ (30/70) selectivity for JUC-220 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ was also calculated to be l5.9 at 1 bar which is higher than SNNU-Bai68 (9.2) [52] and SNNU-Bai69 (6.8) [65].These results indicate that JUC-220 is one of the benchmark materials for alkane separation. It is worth noting that HKUST-1, tbo-MOF-2, and tbo-MOF-3 were also reported to have the abilities of selectively adsorbing $\mathrm{C_{2}H}_{6}$ or $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ which are moderate in MOFs, although they have large pore sizes (Table S8 in the ESM)[15,66]. Therefore, the outstanding selective adsorption of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ or $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ for JUC-220 was probably owing to its pore feature of HKUST-like structure and suitable pore size. In order to properly examine the potential of JUC-220 for adsorptive separations of these light hydrocarbons in pressure swing adsorption (PSA） devices, breakthrough calculations were performed with an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C H_{4}}$ mixture in an adsorber bed packed respectively, using the methodology described in earlier publications [67-7l]. The results showed that JUC-220 has significantly high production capacities for use in practice (Fig.S4l in the ESM). Furthermore, we also calculated the adsorption selectivities of JUC-220 for equimolar gas mixtures of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}},$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}},$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ at 298 and $273\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figs. S39 and S40 in the ESM) as well as the gas mixture of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ with $1/99$ ratio. The adsorption selectivities of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ (1/99 and 50/50) at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ were 2.05 and 2.1 respectively (Fig.4(d) and Fig. S39 in the ESM), indicating its separation ability of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}.$
## 3 Conclusions
In conclusion, according to the geometric similarity of SBF and Cu2 SBUs in HKUST-1,we designed a HKUST-like MOF by substitution of inorganic clusters for organic segments, and synthesized a copper-organic framework, JUC-220. The crystals of JUC-220 showed characteristic features in cubic with disorder, possibly due to the disorder substitution and high symmetry of tbo topology. The novel tbo-MOF exhibited permanent porosities and micropores with prominent pore size around $5.3\mathrm{\textrm{\AA}}$ Gas sorption behavior of JUC-220 for $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}},$ $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}},$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}},$ $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was studied. The results showed that JUC-220 has highly selective gas adsorption abilities for $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ In addition, JUC-220 exhibited trace adsorption of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ and reverse adsorption behavior of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ at room temperature. Future work will include the characterization for more structural information of JUC-220 as well as more instances of the substitution strategy since the paddlewheel SBUs are common in the structure of porous MOFs for gas storage and separation.
